course: dictionary
lesson: 1359
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have several meanings. The words around it show you which meaning the writer wants. Look for the clues. «The crushed cane stems left over are burned to power the sugar mills.» Crushed stems and sugar mills tell you cane means the long, hollow stem of a plant.
explain: Cane can also be a thing you lean on. A cane helps someone walk when their legs are weak or hurt. A white cane is used by people who cannot see well, so others know to give them space. Think about what the sentence is about before you choose a meaning.
explain: Cane can be a word for a thin stick or a strip of plant material too. «That is why the shiny metal saxophone is a woodwind (it uses a reed, a thin vibrating strip of cane).» The bracket explains the reed, so cane must mean the plant material it is made from. Always swap your meaning into the sentence and check that it still makes sense.
fact: Cane is a noun and a verb: a cane can be a walking stick, a plant stem or a sweet, and to cane once meant to hit someone with a stick.
fact: Sugar cane is a tall plant. Its stems are crushed to make sugar, and «the crushed cane stems left over are burned to power the sugar mills».
fact: A white cane helps blind people walk safely, and it tells others that the person is blind or partly sighted.
fact: A candy cane is a striped sweet shaped like a walking stick, often eaten at Christmas.
fact: To cane meant to hit a pupil with a stick as punishment, but this is now banned.
exercise: «The crushed cane stems left over are burned to power the sugar mills.» What is the cane in this sentence? | A tool for hitting someone | *The long hollow stem of a plant | A striped sweet | A stick for walking
exercise: Ruth's grandma uses a white cane when she walks to the shops. What does the white cane help her do? | Keep her hands warm | Carry her shopping home | Sing loudly in the street | *Walk safely and show others she is blind or partly sighted
exercise: «He packs bananas, sugar-cane and melons, says goodbye, and walks across Africa.» What kind of thing is sugar-cane in this list? | A type of hat | A kind of animal | *A plant that people eat | A musical instrument
test: «That is why the shiny metal saxophone is a woodwind (it uses a reed, a thin vibrating strip of cane).» What is the reed made of, according to this sentence? | Glass | *Cane | Wool | Metal
test: In the past, a teacher could cane a pupil. What did cane mean in that sentence? | *To hit someone with a stick as punishment | To buy sweets for the class | To sing loudly | To help a plant grow
exam: Dad tied the tomato plant to a cane so it would stand up straight. Which meaning of cane fits this sentence? | A stem that is crushed to make sugar | A striped sweet shaped like a walking stick | *A thin stick for supporting garden plants | To hit someone with a stick as punishment
